Project Proposal: Porting ripgrep to Twizzler OS

This proposal outlines the plan to port the Rust-based ripgrep (rg) CLI utility to Twizzler OS. This effort serves as a critical stress test for the mlibc POSIX compatibility layer, specifically targeting multithreading and memory-mapping capabilities within Twizzler's unique object-oriented architecture.
Motivation

To achieve a self-hosted development cycle on Twizzler, the environment requires standard, high-performance tooling.

Why ripgrep?

    Stress Test: It aggressively utilizes pthreads and mmap.

    Verification: Porting it proves that our mlibc translation layer can handle complex, concurrent Rust applications.

    Validation: Ensures standard POSIX paths resolve correctly to Twizzler Object IDs through the runtime.

Guide-level Explanation
User Experience

Users will be able to execute search queries in the Twizzler shell using standard syntax:
Bash

rg <pattern> [path]

The behavior will mirror the Linux experience, providing fast, recursive searches across Twizzler objects.
Developer Experience

This project serves as a blueprint for porting complex external crates. Instead of rewriting tools from scratch, developers will:

    Clone the external source into the workspace.

    Compile against the Twizzler target.

    Map missing C library functions to Twizzler runtime APIs.

Reference-level Explanation

The implementation is divided into four key technical milestones:
1. Workspace Integration

    Add ripgrep to the [workspace.members] in the root Cargo.toml.

    Include the resulting binary in the initrd configuration to ensure it is available at boot.

2. File I/O & Path Mapping

    Identify missing POSIX calls during the compilation phase.

    Implement shims in sysdeps.cpp for functions like open and stat.

    Bridge these calls to twz_rt_* APIs to translate standard file paths into Twizzler object references.

3. Memory & Concurrency

    Memory Mapping: Verify that mmap correctly maps Twizzler object pages into the process address space.

    Threading: Ensure pthread_create calls map accurately to the Twizzler thread spawner.

4. Terminal Interface

    Implement dummy ioctl stubs in sysdeps.cpp.

    Return safe defaults for window size and color support to prevent crashes when ripgrep queries terminal properties.

Rationale and Alternatives

    Alternative: Develop a native Twizzler search utility from scratch.

    Rationale against: Writing a native tool is excessively time-consuming. Leveraging ripgrep allows us to provide a world-class tool immediately while simultaneously hardening our compatibility layer.

Prior Art

This project scales the methodology used for uuhelper, which successfully ported basic coreutils (such as ls and cat) to Twizzler. ripgrep represents the next evolution in complexity, moving from simple I/O to heavy concurrency.
Unresolved Questions

    Memory Protection: Does our current mmap implementation support the full range of memory protection flags (PROT_EXEC, PROT_GROWSDOWN, etc.) that ripgrep might expect?

Future Possibilities

If ripgrep runs successfully, it confirms that our mlibc layer is robust enough for high-performance software. This clears the technical debt required to port complex terminal-based editors such as Vim or Helix, bringing Twizzler closer to a full-fledged development environment.
